[mali-g2] Release command buffer memory by an estimate of what the driver keeps, and two small per-call leaks.

The Mali driver keeps host memory for every vkCmdBeginRendering (about 420 bytes) recorded into
command pool memory that has held a query (timestamp, occlusion, ..., in any command buffer of the
pool, even once) since the pool last released, until the pool is reset with RELEASE_RESOURCES,
trimmed or destroyed (clients that reset single command buffers: that buffer's reset with
RELEASE_RESOURCES); resets with flags 0 and the implicit reset of vkBeginCommandBuffer never give it
back. DXVK resets its command pools with flags 0 and records the game's D3D11 queries into the same
command buffers as its rendering, so a Unity game's server grew by ~85 MB a minute until Scudo could
no longer map memory (abort in malloc under vkBeginCommandBuffer). VkRenderPass instances
(vkCmdBeginRenderPass/2) keep nothing, nor does a pool whose only query command is
vkCmdResetQueryPool (tests/frame_leak_test.c).

By default the server now estimates, per command pool of the connection, what the driver keeps
(mali-g2/vortek/src/cmd_memory.h): the command batches replayed into a command buffer are scanned for
vkCmdBeginRendering and query writes; a query marks the pool until it releases, and at
vkEndCommandBuffer a recording into a marked pool adds 420 bytes per vkCmdBeginRendering to its
buffer and pool (counting only recordings with a query of their own missed DXVK's HUD frame-time
timestamp: 12.9 MB a minute on the device). A vkResetCommandPool / vkResetCommandBuffer adds
RELEASE_RESOURCES when the pool's estimate reaches VORTEK_CMD_RELEASE_BYTES (default 16m) or the
connection's reaches VORTEK_CMD_RELEASE_TOTAL_BYTES (default 2 x that) and the pool (buffer) holds
at least 1/16 of the smaller limit (idle pools, such as DXVK's recycled command lists, would
otherwise make the active ones release at every reset); after a vkResetCommandPool with flags 0 the
memory is the pool's (releasing or freeing single buffers then gives nothing back), so the pool's
buffers no longer carry shares of it; vkBeginCommandBuffer of a buffer due for release in a pool
that allows single resets resets it with RELEASE_RESOURCES first, unless the client reset it
explicitly (with flags 0 the memory is the pool's then). Pools without queries never release; marked
pools release at least at every 4096th reset. VORTEK_CMD_RELEASE_LOG=1 logs every release (otherwise the first four and every 64th). With
VORTEK_CMD_RELEASE_EVERY set to a number, every Nth reset of the same pool or command buffer releases
as before (1 = always, 0 = never; a 64-entry table per connection, a new or colliding handle
releases) and nothing is counted; any other value is ignored (logged). The estimate's
tables have fixed sizes; when one is full, the server falls back to every 32nd reset.
Tests: tests/frame_leak_test.c, tests/frame_leak_test.sh.

vkWaitSemaphores (timeline_semaphore.c) and the async pipeline creation unserialize into a
MemoryPool without a backing buffer, and vt_free() frees the chunks but not the allocation list's
array: 32 bytes leaked per call.

